  "id": "chinese_opera",
  "name": "Chinese Opera (xiqu regional traditions)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Umbrella tradition of some three hundred Chinese sung-theatre forms descending from Yuan zaju and Ming kunqu, standardized regionally from the eighteenth century onward — jingju in Beijing after the 1790 Anhui troupes, yueju in Shanghai and Guangdong, chuanju in Sichuan, yuju in Henan — built on role-type vocal production (sheng, dan, jing, chou), a percussion-led ensemble whose bangu clapper-drum leader cues every entrance and gesture, bowed jinghu or erhu carrying the melodic line in fixed banqiang metrical types, and highly ornamented, nasal-forward, non-vibrato singing over pentatonic gong-based modes.",
